  <article class="blog-post">
    <header class="article-header">
      <h1>Maine Home Grow Laws: Adult-Use vs Medical Rules</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">A source-based comparison of Maine's two separate home-cultivation frameworks</p>
      <div class="byline">
        <p>By <a href="/about/authors#margaret-finch"><strong>Margaret Finch</strong></a>, Finance &amp; Taxation Analyst. <strong>Reviewed by <a href="/about/authors#thalia-greene">Thalia Greene</a>, Cultivation &amp; Horticulture Reviewer.</strong></p>
        <p class="last-updated">Published 2026-04-18 · Last reviewed 2026-07-16</p>
      </div>
      <p class="affiliate-top-disclosure"><strong>Affiliate disclosure:</strong> This article contains affiliate links. If you purchase through one, Maine Dispensary Guide may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. See the <a href="#affiliate-section">full disclosure below</a>.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="answer-capsule">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Maine allows home cultivation under two different laws. Adults 21 and older use the personal adult-use rules in 28-B M.R.S. §1502. Qualifying medical patients use the separate Maine Medical Use of Cannabis Act in 22 M.R.S. §2423-A. Both frameworks generally use a 6-mature/12-immature/unlimited-seedling allocation, but their cultivation-area, tagging, possession, designation, and access rules are not interchangeable.
    </div>

    <section id="which-rules-apply">
      <h2>Which Maine Grow Rules Apply to You?</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>You are 21+ and growing for personal adult use:</strong> start with <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Title 28-B §1502</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>You are a qualifying medical patient growing for yourself:</strong> use <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Title 22 §2423-A</a> and read the <a href="/blog/maine-medical-cannabis-patient-grow-guide">medical patient grow guide</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>You are growing for another patient:</strong> caregiver designation and registration rules may apply; do not rely on the ordinary adult-use rules.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="comparison">
      <h2>Adult-Use and Medical Patient Home Grow Compared</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table aria-label="Comparison of Maine personal adult-use and qualifying medical patient cultivation rules">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Personal adult use</th><th scope="col">Qualifying medical patient</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Controlling law</td><td><a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §1502</a>; it expressly excludes medical cultivation.</td><td><a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">22 M.R.S. §2423-A</a>, Maine Medical Use of Cannabis Act.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Who qualifies</td><td>A person age 21 or older cultivating for personal adult use. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502 introductory text and sub-§1</a>.</td><td>A qualifying patient under the medical act. <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(1)</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plant allocation</td><td>Up to 6 mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings per person. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(1)</a>.</td><td>Up to a total of 6 mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings for that patient—a combined allocation whether cultivated by the patient or by a caregiver. <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(1)(B)</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Can a caregiver grow for the person?</td><td>No caregiver pathway exists under the personal adult-use provision. That section excludes cultivation authorized by the medical act. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502 introductory text</a>.</td><td>Yes, if properly designated; some or all of the same patient allocation may be cultivated by a caregiver. Do not add a separate six plants for the same patient. <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(1)(B) and (F-1)</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Required grow location/area</td><td>On land where the grower is domiciled, land the grower owns, or other land with the owner's written agreement; multiple locations are allowed if all requirements are met. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(1)(A)-(C)</a>.</td><td>A patient who elects to cultivate must keep plants in a cultivation area except during authorized transport; access is limited as the statute specifies. <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(3)(A)</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plant tags</td><td>Every mature and immature plant needs a legible tag containing the statutory fields. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(2)(C)</a>.</td><td>Do not assume the adult-use tag rule applies; consult the <a href={medicalRule} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">current MMCP rule/OCP</a>. The controlling patient provisions cited here do not establish the adult-use tag fields for patient plants.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Harvested-cannabis possession</td><td>The grower may possess all cannabis produced by the authorized plants at the residence or cultivation location; the general away-from-grow limit is separate. <a href={adultUsePossessionLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §1501(1)(B), (E)</a>.</td><td>Up to 8 pounds of harvested cannabis. <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(1)(A)</a>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Municipal/property caveat</td><td>Local rules may regulate a grow, but a municipality may not generally prohibit personal adult-use cultivation and must preserve the statutory domiciled-adult floor. Property ownership and permission conditions still matter. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(1), (3)</a>.</td><td>Medical cultivation follows its own statute, current MMCP rules, and any applicable property or local requirements; verify the current facts with <a href={ocpFaq} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OCP</a>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="source-note">This table is a route-selection aid, not a substitute for reading the controlling law. A patient's own six-plant ceiling and a caregiver's cultivation for that same patient should not be treated as two allocations to stack. This page deliberately does not resolve stacking across other separate legal capacities. Ask OCP or a Maine attorney about your exact facts.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="definitions">
      <h2>Definitions That Control the Plant Count</h2>
      <p>These labels are legal categories, not casual gardening shorthand. Title 28-B defines a <strong>mature plant</strong> as a flowering cannabis plant, an <strong>immature plant</strong> as a plant that is neither mature nor a seedling, and a <strong>seedling</strong> as a nonflowering plant or rooted cutting that is less than 24 inches high and less than 24 inches wide. See <a href={adultUseDefinitionsLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §102-A(32), (41), and (59)</a>.</p>
      <p>For the medical program, a <strong>cultivation area</strong> is an indoor or outdoor area used to grow mature plants, immature plants, or seedlings; it may contain multiple contiguous or noncontiguous areas on the same parcel. A <strong>qualifying patient</strong> is a Maine resident of at least 30 days who has a valid written certification, subject to the full statutory definition. See <a href={medicalDefinitionsLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">22 M.R.S. §2421-A(17) and (39)</a>. Title 22 separately defines mature plants, immature plants, and seedlings for medical-program use; do not move a definition from one program into the other without checking the controlling title.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="adult-use-rules">
      <h2>Personal Adult-Use Cultivation: The Compliance Sequence</h2>
      <h3>1. Count plants by growth stage</h3>
      <p>A person 21 or older may cultivate up to <strong>6 mature cannabis plants, 12 immature cannabis plants, and an unlimited number of seedlings</strong> for personal adult use, subject to the rest of §1502. The allocation is per qualifying person, not an invitation to combine plants without tracking who cultivates each one. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §1502(1)</a>.</p>

      <h3>2. Use an allowed parcel</h3>
      <p>The statute allows cultivation on the parcel where the person is domiciled, on a parcel the person owns but does not live on, or on land the person neither owns nor lives on when the owner gives written permission. A grow may span multiple locations only if every location complies. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(1)(A)-(C)</a>.</p>

      <h3>3. Keep the grow out of unaided view from a public way</h3>
      <p>Cannabis must not be visible from a public way without aircraft, binoculars, or another optical aid. Evaluate the actual sight line as plants grow; the statute states the result required, not a particular fence design. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(2)(A)</a>.</p>

      <h3>4. Prevent access by people under 21</h3>
      <p>The grower must take reasonable precautions against unauthorized access by a person under 21. What is reasonable depends on the site, so use security appropriate to the household and location rather than treating concealment alone as enough. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(2)(B)</a>.</p>

      <h3>5. Tag every mature and immature plant</h3>
      <p>Each mature and immature plant must carry a legible tag with the grower's name; driver's-license or identification number; a notation that the plant is grown for personal adult use under §1502; and, when cultivation is on land owned by another person, that owner's name. The statutory tag rule does not list seedlings. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(2)(C)</a>.</p>

      <h3>6. Check local rules, but know the statutory floor</h3>
      <p>A municipality may regulate personal adult-use home cultivation and may limit total mature plants on a parcel. Its rule must still allow each domiciled adult age 21 or older to cultivate 6 mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings. A municipality may not generally ban personal adult-use home cultivation, zone it out of all areas, or charge the grower a home-cultivation license fee. Parallel rules apply through LUPC in unorganized and deorganized areas. <a href={adultUseLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§1502(3)-(4)</a>.</p>

      <h3>7. Keep the harvest where the broader allowance applies</h3>
      <p>Title 28-B permits possession of all cannabis produced by an authorized home grow <strong>at the person's residence or at the cultivation location</strong>. That is different from the general authorization to possess or transport up to 2½ ounces, including no more than 10 grams of concentrate. <a href={adultUsePossessionLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">28-B M.R.S. §1501(1)(B), (E)</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="medical-summary">
      <h2>Qualifying Medical Patients: A Separate, Bounded Rule</h2>
      <p>The medical law is not an expanded version of §1502. Under <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">22 M.R.S. §2423-A(1)(B)</a>, a qualifying patient may cultivate—or designate a caregiver operating under the cited provisions to cultivate—up to a total of 6 mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings for that patient. The mature and immature limits are combined patient allocations <strong>whether cultivated by the patient or by a caregiver</strong>.</p>
      <p>A patient who grows must keep the plants in a cultivation area except during authorized transport, and access is limited to the patient plus the specifically listed supervised or emergency access in <a href={medicalLaw} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§2423-A(3)(A)</a>. Caregiver business limits such as 30 mature plants/60 immature plants or canopy alternatives are separate caregiver provisions—not a patient's personal allowance.</p>
      <p>For designation, cultivation-area, household, and caregiver distinctions, use the <a href="/blog/maine-medical-cannabis-patient-grow-guide">Maine medical cannabis patient grow guide</a>. Do not assume adult-use tagging, location, or possession rules carry over.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="common-scenarios">
      <h2>Common Scenarios and Mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>One person has more than one legal status:</strong> the reviewed statutes do not provide a general formula for stacking adult-use, patient, and caregiver allotments. Do not add them together without current OCP or Maine legal guidance.</li>
        <li><strong>A patient and caregiver split cultivation:</strong> they share the patient's combined 6-mature/12-immature allocation; the caregiver does not receive a second allocation for that patient.</li>
        <li><strong>Household members share a room:</strong> shared cultivation areas do not pool or increase individual or per-patient limits. Keep records showing whose plants are whose.</li>
        <li><strong>An adult-use harvest leaves the residence or grow site:</strong> the broad crop-possession allowance applies at those locations; away from them, the general possession and transport limits remain separate.</li>
        <li><strong>A tenant grows at the tenant's domicile:</strong> §1502 does not by itself resolve every lease or property-right question. Review the lease, building and fire rules, insurance or federal-housing conditions, and obtain current legal guidance rather than assuming either unrestricted cultivation or a statutory written-permission requirement.</li>
        <li><strong>A patient copies an adult-use tag:</strong> do not assume §1502's adult-use tag fields apply to patient plants. Check the current MMCP rule and OCP guidance for the medical framework.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

