          question: "How many cannabis plants can I grow in Maine?",
          answer: "For personal adult use, a person age 21 or older may cultivate up to 6 mature plants, 12 immature plants, and unlimited seedlings under 28-B M.R.S. §1502. A qualifying patient's separate medical allocation is generally the same count for that patient under 22 M.R.S. §2423-A, whether cultivated by the patient or a designated caregiver. First identify which law applies.",
        },
        {
          question: "Are Maine's medical and recreational home-grow rules the same?",
          answer: "No. Section 1502 expressly excludes medical cultivation. Although the patient and personal adult-use allocations generally share the same numbers, their cultivation-area, access, designation, tagging, location, and possession rules are not interchangeable.",
        },
        {
          question: "Can a Maine medical patient and caregiver each grow six mature plants for the same patient?",
          answer: "Do not treat the limits as two separate six-plant allowances for one patient. Section 2423-A(1)(B) caps that patient's total at 6 mature and 12 immature plants whether cultivated by the patient or by the caregiver. This page does not resolve stacking across other legal capacities; ask OCP or a Maine attorney about the exact arrangement.",
        },
        {
          question: "Do adult-use plants need tags?",
          answer: "Yes. Under 28-B M.R.S. §1502(2)(C), each mature and immature adult-use plant needs a legible tag with the grower's name, driver's-license or ID number, a personal-adult-use notation, and the landowner's name when the grow is on land owned by another person.",
        },
        {
          question: "Can a landlord or property owner restrict cannabis cultivation?",
          answer: "Property rights and private agreements still matter. Section 1502 expressly requires a written owner agreement when the grower neither owns nor is domiciled on the parcel, and requires the owner's name on adult-use plant tags whenever another person owns the land. For a tenant growing at the tenant's domicile, §1502 does not by itself resolve every lease or property-right question. Review the lease, building and fire rules, insurance or federal-housing conditions, and obtain current legal guidance rather than assuming either unrestricted cultivation or a statutory written-permission requirement.",
        },
        {
          question: "Can a Maine town ban all personal adult-use cultivation?",
          answer: "Not generally. Section 1502(3) allows municipal regulation but says a municipality may not generally prohibit personal adult-use home cultivation, zone it out of all areas, or charge a home-grow license fee. A parcel cap must preserve the statutory allocation for each adult age 21 or older domiciled there.",
        },
        {
          question: "Can I take home-grown cannabis across state lines?",
          answer: "No. Maine OCP states that it is illegal to leave Maine with cannabis products, whether medical or recreational, and that cannabis may not be mailed from Maine. State authorization does not permit interstate transport.",
